@page "/form-select"

<CatHead Title="Selects">
    <code>.form-select</code> is the whole class list a dropdown needs &mdash; it carries the same field
    shape <code>.form-input</code> does, from one rule the two names share. It styles the
    <em>panel</em> as well as the box, so the option list takes the theme.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-information-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Two selects behind one class</span>
        <code>appearance: base-select</code> takes the option list out of the platform's hands and makes it
        a real panel in the page. A browser without it keeps the platform list and gets a caret painted
        from two gradients instead &mdash; the two halves are exclusive, behind <code>@@supports</code>,
        and neither is a script or a shim. A list box has no caret either way, since there is nothing to
        drop.
    </div>
</div>

<CatGroup Title="Drop-downs">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.Dropdown" Title="Plain select" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>A plain <code>&lt;select class="form-select"&gt;</code>. Nothing has to be added to the markup.</p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.Placement" Title="List that opens upward" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live.</strong> The drop-down opens below the select. In a bar at the foot of a pane,
            <code>--panel-placement: block-start</code> on the bar opens it above, and
            <code>inline-end</code> or <code>inline-start</code> beside it &mdash; the token every
            anchored panel reads, with the <a href="/menu#where-it-opens">menu's</a> four values. A list
            placed above or beside turns round where that side has no room; one below stays below and
            scrolls inside itself.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.OptionContent" Title="Options with content" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            An <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> may hold real markup &mdash; an icon, a second line, a trailing
            code. <strong>Add a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> holding
            <code>&lt;selectedcontent&gt;&lt;/selectedcontent&gt;</code> as the select's first child</strong>
            and the closed box shows a clone of whichever option is chosen. Leave it out and the browser
            supplies its own, which runs every text node together. A browser without base
            appearance — Safari — shows the option's whole text in the list and in the closed
            box, so the <code>.option-sep</code> between label and hint is what keeps it
            readable there: <code>Frankfurt · eu-central-1</code>. Base appearance hides it.
        </p>
        <p>
            The clone is the browser's to make and Blazor's render does not trigger it, so
            <code>Sedna.UI.js</code> repairs it on load and after every render &mdash;
            <code>sednaUi.select.refresh()</code> on the <a href="/script">script page</a>. The submitted
            value is the option's text run together and trimmed, so keep <code>value</code> explicit.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.Groups" Title="Groups and separators" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Give a group a <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> as well as its <code>label</code>. The attribute's
            heading is drawn by the browser outside this document and cannot be themed &mdash; it is what a
            fallback browser shows, and the <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> is what this one does.
        </p>
        <p>
            Do not put an <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code> between groups. The second group draws the one line that
            divides them, and a browser that separates its own groups draws its own; an <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>
            on top of either is a second line. Keep <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code> for loose options that belong to
            no group.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="List boxes">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.LibraryListbox" Title="Listbox" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.listbox</code> is the library's own list box: a <code>role="listbox"</code> box of
            <code>role="option"</code> rows the app writes, drawn the same in every browser.
            <code>Sedna.UI.js</code> is the keyboard and the choice &mdash; arrows, <kbd>Home</kbd>,
            <kbd>End</kbd>, typeahead, <kbd>Space</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<kbd>A</kbd>
            &mdash; and writes <code>aria-selected</code>, then sends <code>change</code> and
            <code>sedna-listbox-select</code> with the values. It is one tab stop, the way a
            <a href="/form-combo">combo field</a> is.
        </p>
        <p>
            A chosen row is a brand <strong>tint</strong> as well as brand text, with a tick at the trailing
            edge, so several chosen rows read as a set and not by colour alone. Rows are a step apart, so a
            chosen row and the one the pointer is on never touch. A listbox is not a form control and posts
            nothing: where a form must carry the choice, use the native list box below.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.ListBoxGroups" Title="Sections in a listbox" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Rows in <code>role="group"</code>s, each named by a <code>.listbox-heading</code>. Whole lists are on
            screen, so the headings are what stop forty rows reading as one column.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.ListBoxContent" Title="Rows with content" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.option-text</code> and <code>.option-hint</code>, as in the dropdown above. A listbox keeps the
            second line where a closed select drops it, because nothing is closed.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.Transfer" Title="Moving rows between two lists" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; pick a row and press the arrow.</strong> For choosing a set out of a long list,
            where a column of checkboxes is unreadable and a list alone never answers <em>what have I chosen so
            far</em>. The right-hand list is that answer.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>.transfer</code> is the three-column grid and <code>.transfer-actions</code> the column
            of buttons; <code>data-transfer</code> plus <code>data-transfer-from</code> /
            <code>-to</code> / <code>-add</code> / <code>-remove</code> is what
            <code><a href="/script">sednaUi.transfer</a></code> reads, and the two lists may be
            <code>.listbox</code>es, as here, or <code>&lt;select multiple&gt;</code>s. Both lists get a bubbling
            <code>change</code> after every move, and insertion is alphabetical only where the destination was
            already sorted: re-ordering a list whose order means something would destroy it silently.
        </p>
        <p>
            Choosing is a <em>staging</em> act &mdash; these are the ones I am about to move &mdash; and the answer is
            which list a row ends up in. So nothing arrives chosen and the destination's own choice is cleared:
            rows that landed chosen accumulated into a highlight the reader never made. The first row moved is
            scrolled into view instead.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.ListBox" Title="Native list box" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A <code>&lt;select multiple&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;select size="4"&gt;</code> with
            <code>.form-select</code>, for when a form must post the choice. Two different controls the CSS treats
            alike: <code>multiple</code> takes several answers, <code>size</code> on its own still takes one and
            merely shows the options without opening.
        </p>
        <p>
            Where <code>appearance: base-select</code> exists (Chromium) the rows are themed like a
            dropdown's: a brand tint, the tick at the trailing edge, a step between rows. <strong>Safari draws a
            native list box</strong> whose selection colour, row height and spacing no CSS reaches, so the same
            markup looks like the operating system there. Where the look must be the same everywhere, use the
            <code>.listbox</code> above.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="States and sizes">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.States" Title="Disabled and required" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A disabled <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> should say why in its own text, or it reads as a bug. A
            <code>required</code> select needs an empty first option, or the first real one counts as an
            answer the reader never gave.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.form-select--sm</code> and <code>-lg</code> &mdash; the select's own tier, not the
            input's, because a painted caret needs its room back at every size.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormSelect.InAToolbar" Title="Beside a filter field" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.sedna-w-fit</code> is what keeps a filter dropdown the width of its value instead of the
            width of the bar. The heights agree because the tiers do.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
